<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taos, New Mexico</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taos is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> beside Taos Pueblo, a multi-storey adobe village inhabited for about a thousand years and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Under Spanish rule its annual trade fairs drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a>, Ute and other Plains peoples to exchange horses, captives and hides for goods. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680 was planned partly at Taos.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taos-new-mexico.jpg" alt="Taos, New Mexico" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zeality · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taos_plaza_la_fonda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Taos lies in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> between the Rio Grande Gorge and the Sangre de Cristo Mountains, about 50 miles south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> border, and is the county seat of Taos County. Its name comes from the Taos language and means place of red willows. To the north lies Taos Pueblo, a multi-storey adobe village, in places five storeys high, that has been occupied for nearly a thousand years and was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1992. It is the most northern of the New Mexico pueblos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Spanish colonists founded Taos around 1615 as Don Fernando de Taos. Relations with the pueblo were at first amicable, but resentment of meddling missionaries and of tribute demanded by encomenderos led to a revolt in 1640, in which Taos people killed their priest and several settlers and left the pueblo, not returning until 1661. In 1680 Taos Pueblo joined the widespread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a>. After the Spanish reconquest of 1692 it kept up armed resistance until 1696, when Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-vargas">Diego de Vargas</a> defeated its people in Taos Canyon. In the 1770s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanches</a> raided Taos repeatedly, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-de-anza">Juan Bautista de Anza</a> led a punitive expedition against them in 1779.</p><p>Governor Fernando Chacón established Taos as a permanent settlement in 1795, a fortified plaza and trading outpost, and a land grant of 1796 and 1797 gave land to 63 Spanish families. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> in 1847, Hispanics and American Indians in Taos rose in the Taos Revolt, in which the newly appointed governor, Charles Bent, was killed on 19 January 1847. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> ceded the region in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a> in 1848, and New Mexico became a state in 1912. The American flag has flown continuously over the plaza since the Civil War, when the officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kit-carson">Kit Carson</a> had it guarded against Confederate sympathisers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Artists began to settle in Taos at the end of the 19th century, and six of them formed the Taos Society of Artists in 1915. They painted local scenes, often modelled by people from the pueblo, and their houses and studios, among them the Ernest L. Blumenschein House and the Nicolai Fechin House, are listed on the National Register of Historic Places. Later residents included Georgia O'Keeffe, D. H. Lawrence and Dennis Hopper.</p><p>The town was incorporated in 1934 and holds more than twenty sites on the National Register of Historic Places. Each July the Fiestas de Taos honour its patron saints, Santa Ana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, on the plaza.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taos, New Mexico” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
